    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The episodic uplift of anticlines above blind thrust faults indicates the important mechanical interaction between seismic events on these faults and the development of their associated folds. An examination of secondary deformation, such as bedding-plane faults or extensional fractures, induced by the elevated stress state over a blind thrust, may provide a framework for understanding fractures and faults in folds. The near-tip stress concentrations of the main fault, which control fault propagation, are influenced by the deformation of secondary features. Results obtained from a numerical model using the boundary element method for displacement on faults and frictional interfaces show that slip on secondary surfaces above blind thrust faults may promote fault propagation. Additional relationships suggest that blind thrust faults may intersect and propagate along bedding-plane faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
